LAST_SUCCESS=Task 117 M2 step 2c DONE — software H.265 via oxideav-h265 (pure-Rust MIT), wired into the host. bbb-h265.mp4 (1280x720, real B-frames): 300/300 decoded, presentation order OK, snapshot PIXEL-CORRECT. Uses ONLY the oxideav-h265 codec crate (bypasses oxideav registry + unfinished HW). Host: video::Codec::H265 + video/hevc mime, codec2b/codec_of, openh264-style adapter, --video-decode-file parses hvcC. Both platforms build (Android=MediaCodec HEVC HW).
NOTES=CAUGHT a latent PTS-mispairing bug: oxideav-h265 doc says take_decoded=display order but measured POC 0,5,3,1,2,4 = DECODE order; pop-min pairing would silently desync A/V while passing no-loss. Fixed to FIFO (like openh264), verified by reading POC. PERF: oxideav-h265 ~6fps @720p on i7-8565U = NOT real-time (paced player drops most frames); decode is CORRECT, just slow -> HEVC real-time needs HW. Codecs now: VP8/VP9(libvpx), H.264(openh264), H.265(oxideav-h265) all decode real files on desktop. NEXT: HW backends for real-time — VAAPI (fedora confirmed: vainfo H264 VLD, va_openDriver=0; oxideav vaapi is 🚧/broken so we write our own) OR adopt oxideav-av1 SW decoder next. repros/h264-mp4-decode now does both H.264 and H.265.
